        "date":  "2026-09-15",
        "theme":  "Strategy",
        "summary":  "AI hyperscalers are investing over $1 trillion annually in data centers, yet current AI revenues stand at only $150-200 billion yearly. To justify these expenditures, companies must achieve 2.7x productivity growth by 2030 while generating trillions in future revenue — or risk the investments becoming stranded assets if the technology fails to deliver promised economic returns.",
        "execSummary":  "MIT Technology Review quantifies the structural mismatch at the heart of the AI boom: $1 trillion in annual infrastructure spend against $150-200 billion in current revenue, requiring unprecedented productivity gains to avoid a bubble collapse. For enterprise AI strategy, this macroeconomic context is directly relevant — if the infrastructure bet fails to pay out, vendor pricing, model availability, and cloud AI services could destabilize significantly in the 2028-2030 window. CIOs should factor infrastructure concentration risk into multi-year AI roadmaps and avoid single-vendor dependencies that would be acutely exposed to a market correction. The implication is that enterprises building on AI infrastructure should favor portable, standards-based architectures over proprietary lock-in.",
